1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,793
What if Earth were tipped on its side, like Uranus?

2
00:00:03,283 --> 00:00:06,165
On November 25, Uranus reached opposition.

3
00:00:06,165 --> 00:00:08,635
It's as bright as it gets this year.

4
00:00:08,635 --> 00:00:12,134
Easy in binoculars, from Taurus, near the Pleiades.

5
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:16,224
Uranus spins tipped over by about ninety-eight degrees.

6
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:19,083
NASA says a giant collision may be to blame.

7
00:00:19,583 --> 00:00:21,615
Its year lasts eighty-four of ours.

8
00:00:21,615 --> 00:00:24,226
So each pole gets years of sunshine overhead,

9
00:00:24,226 --> 00:00:27,708
while the other half sits in a 21-year winter night.

10
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:29,959
Like and subscribe for more what-ifs.

11
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:32,597
Tip Earth the same way, and in June,

12
00:00:32,597 --> 00:00:35,759
almost the whole northern half gets nonstop daylight,

13
00:00:35,759 --> 00:00:38,145
while the south sits in months of night.

14
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:39,471
The surprise?

15
00:00:39,471 --> 00:00:44,148
One climate model found deep enough oceans could keep a tipped world mild,

16
00:00:44,148 --> 00:00:45,285
even at the poles.

17
00:00:45,783 --> 00:00:47,468
So what if Earth had two moons?

18
00:00:47,468 --> 00:00:48,880
Full story on the channel.
